format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

lesson radiation-and-cells
title: Radiation and your cells
minutes: 5
xp: 50
teaches: u4-cell-damage
teaches: u4-cell-repair
relies_on: u3-exposure-vs-contamination

description:
    Radiation can damage the DNA in a cell, yet an everyday dose does not make anyone ill. You will see why, and what repair has to do with it.

cite ref-epa-radiation-health-effects

supports:
    Ionizing radiation can damage the DNA in cells; cells are extremely efficient at repairing it; damage not repaired correctly can kill a cell or eventually make it cancerous.

cite ref-hps-radioactive-contamination
supports: A person who was exposed to radiation is not radioactive.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: A puzzle

body:
    Ionizing radiation carries enough energy to affect the atoms in living cells. That means it can damage a cell's [[dna|DNA]], its genetic material. Yet you get a small dose every day and you are not ill. Why not?

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: What radiation can do to a cell
figure: radiation-hits-cell
figure_at: 1

body:
    When ionizing radiation passes through a cell, it can hit the atoms inside it. If it hits the DNA, the DNA is damaged. The cell has to deal with that damage.

card c03
kind: item
